y $A$ and $B$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"b27ed2962c8496890ff3a38d18f766132088acafb8a3fbf31d8ce2d920231128"},"seconds":36.77185869216919,"seed":890848473404959041,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to interpret and solve the given addition problem in base 7. The given addition problem is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n& & & & A & B_7\\\\\n&+ & & & B & A_7\\\\\n\\cline{2-6}\n& & & A & A & 0_7\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThis can be written as the equation:\n\\[ (A \\cdot 7 + B) + (B \\cdot 7 + A) = A \\cdot 7^2 + A \\cdot 7 + 0 \\]\n\nSimplifying the left side, we get:\n\\[ A \\cdot 7 + B + B \\cdot 7 + A = A \\cdot 49 + A \\cdot 7 \\]\n\\[ (A + B) \\cdot 7 + (A + B) = A \\cdot 49 + A \\cdot 7 \\]\n\\[ (A + B) \\cdot 8 = A \\cdot 49 + A \\cdot 7 \\]\n\\[ (A + B) \\cdot 8 = A \\cdot 56 \\]\n\\[ A + B = 7A \\]\n\\[ B = 6A \\]\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are digits in base 7, they must be between 0 and 6. The only value of \\(A\\) that makes \\(B = 6A\\) a valid digit in base 7 is \\(A = 1\\), which gives \\(B = 6\\).\n\nLet's verify this solution by substituting \\(A = 1\\) and \\(B = 6\\) back into the original addition problem:\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\\;}c@{}c@{}c@{}c}\n& & & & 1 & 6_7\\\\\n&+ & & & 6 & 1_7\\\\\n\\cline{2-6}\n& & & 1 & 1 & 0_7\\\\\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nIn base 10, this is:\n\\[ 1 \\cdot 7 + 6 + 6 \\cdot 7 + 1 = 13 + 43 = 56 \\]\n\\[ 1 \\cdot 49 + 1 \\cdot 7 + 0 = 49 + 7 = 56 \\]\n\nThe addition is correct, so our values for \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are correct. The product of the digits represented by \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) is:\n\\[ 1 \\times 6 = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{6}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
